<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">County Palatine of Chester</h1><p class="st-meta">1071 CE – 1237 CE (approx.) · capital Chester</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Norman marcher earldom on the Welsh border whose earls ruled Cheshire almost as independent princes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After crushing northern and Mercian resistance, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> gave the earldom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerbod-the-fleming">Gerbod the Fleming</a> and then, in 1071, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester">Hugh d'Avranches</a>. Its purpose was to guard the northern Welsh border and push into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Gwynedd</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The earls held nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, with their own courts, sheriff and barons, and royal writs did not normally run there, a status later called palatine. Hugh and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-rhuddlan">Robert of Rhuddlan</a> advanced along the north Welsh coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranulf-de-gernon-4th-earl-of-chester">Ranulf de Gernon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranulf-de-blondeville-6th-earl-of-chester">Ranulf de Blondeville</a> were major figures in the wars of Stephen and John; Ranulf III built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beeston-castle">Beeston Castle</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-scotland-earl-of-huntingdon">John the Scot</a>, the last earl of the line, died childless in 1237, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III</a> took the earldom into royal hands. From 1301 it was given to the heir to the throne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a> kept palatine institutions until 1830.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-palatine-of-chester.jpg" alt="County Palatine of Chester" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_north_west_from_Beeston_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4547395_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1071 CE – 1237 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chester</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugh d'Avranches, Ranulf de Gernon, Ranulf de Blondeville</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anglo-Norman French, Middle English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Earldom of Chester</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1154&amp;focus=county-palatine-of-chester" aria-label="County Palatine of Chester on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4616 347 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County Palatine of Chester lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4936 603L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 603h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 603h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chester. <a href="/atlas?year=1154&amp;focus=county-palatine-of-chester">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
